messages:
tess-docs: Opening a small PR to recommend a single "envelope" convention for tool/function outputs in the structured-output guide. Goal: fewer parser surprises, explicit schema_version, and a simple migration path. Need quick eyeballs on examples + tiny JS/Py shim snippets.

noah-ml: +1. Can you list the problem cases you want to address in the PR body (singleton vs array, omitted keys vs null, and mixed types in arrays)?

tess-docs: Will do. Draft rules in PR:
- Always put data under `envelope.data` and make `data` an array.
- Top-level `envelope.kind` to describe the response (tool_response, embedding, etc.).
- Include `schema_version` and a `meta` object for model provenance.
- Prefer explicit nulls for missing optional fields instead of omitting keys.

omar-devex: We'll need small compatibility shims in SDK docs so older examples don't break. Keep the shims tiny and local to docs (copy-paste friendly).

sylvia-ux: Please include a compact before/after diff and a one-liner explaining why arrays are better for consumers (consistent iteration + easier batching).

tess-docs: Draft before/after to put in the guide:
```json
// OLD (ambiguous)
{ "result": { "text": "ok" } }

// NEW (recommended)
{ "envelope": { "kind": "tool_response", "schema_version": "1.0", "data": [ { "text": "ok" } ], "meta": { "model": "redwood-v2" } } }
```

omar-devex: Here are the shim snippets I dropped in the PR body. JS normalize: 
```js
function normalize(resp) {
  if (resp && resp.envelope && Array.isArray(resp.envelope.data)) return resp.envelope;
  if (Array.isArray(resp)) return { kind: 'legacy_array', schema_version: '0', data: resp };
  if (resp && resp.result) return { kind: 'legacy_result', schema_version: '0', data: Array.isArray(resp.result) ? resp.result : [resp.result] };
  return { kind: 'unknown', schema_version: '0', data: [] };
}
```
Python normalize: 
```py
def normalize(resp):
    if resp and isinstance(resp.get('envelope'), dict) and isinstance(resp['envelope'].get('data'), list):
        return resp['envelope']
    if isinstance(resp, list):
        return {'kind': 'legacy_array', 'schema_version': '0', 'data': resp}
    if resp and 'result' in resp:
        r = resp['result']
        return {'kind': 'legacy_result', 'schema_version': '0', 'data': r if isinstance(r, list) else [r]}
    return {'kind': 'unknown', 'schema_version': '0', 'data': []}
```

sylvia-ux: Nice. Add one tiny consumer snippet showing robust iteration: `for item in envelope['data']:` and a short note about type guards for union items.

noah-ml: Also add guidance for union discriminators: include a `meta.discriminator` entry or require a `type` field on data items. Show a brief example item { "type": "file", "path": "/tmp/x" }.

omar-devex: I'll also add a recommended unit test for SDKs: assert normalize(...)["data"] is a list and items preserve their shape.

merge-bot: PR created: https://github.com/redwood-inference/docs/pull/5018 - "Envelope shape + shims for structured outputs" by tess-docs. Docs build passed.

tess-docs: Thanks all. Plan: land as a recommended pattern (not a forced rewrite). After merge we'll run a link sweep for old `result` examples and submit targeted follow-ups per tutorial to avoid breaking copies. Merge tomorrow unless objections. :rocket:
